=== layout: section

# Timeline of Theranos

=== layout: one-panel

# Timeline of Theranos (1 of 3) - Part 1

@body

- Summer 2002\: Holmes and Balwani meet on a trip to Beijing
- September 2002\: Holmes starts at Stanford
- 2003\: Holmes founds Theranos at age 19
- March 2004\: Holmes drops out of Stanford
- 2004-2008\: Theranos thrives with early funding
- 2009\: Ramesh “Sunny” Balwani joins the company as the number-two executive.
  - Ms. Holmes doesn’t widely disclose that the two, who met the summer before she started college, are also romantically involved.

=== layout: one-panel

# Timeline of Theranos (1 of 3) - Part 2

@body

- 2011\: Former U.S. Secretary of State George Shultz joins Theranos’s board
- September 2013\: Theranos launches its Walgreens partnership
- December 2014\: Elizabeth Holmes delivers a Ted Talk
- September 2014\: Holmes named one of the richest women in America by Forbes
- December 2014\: Theranos has raised \$400 million, Holmes gets article in the New Yorker that tips off John Carreyrou

=== layout: one-panel

# Timeline of Theranos (2 of 3) - Part 1

@body

- February 2015\: Criticism from the medical community for not publishing any of its research
- July 2015\: FDA clears the Theranos HSV-1 antibody test
- October 15, 2015\: Carreyrou’s first damning report
- October 27, 2015\: FDA releases reports and stating that the company had used uncleared and unsuitable medical devices to run its blood tests.
- November 2015\: Safeway deal folds
- December 2015\: Carreyrou strikes again

@notes

FDA clearance source: https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/cdrh/cfdocs/cfpmn/pmn.cfm?ID=K143236
Clearance of this one assay did not establish the performance of all Theranos tests.

=== layout: one-panel

# Timeline of Theranos (2 of 3) - Part 2

@body

- January 2016\: lab inspection uncovered deficient practices that pose immediate jeopardy to patient health and safety
- May 2016\: Balwani steps down; Theranos voids two years of blood tests
- June 2016\: Holmes net worth revised to \$0; Theranos loses Walgreens
- July 2016\: Holmes is banned from running labs for two years
- October 2016\: Theranos closes its medical lab operations amid regulatory scrutiny

@notes

FDA clearance source: https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/cdrh/cfdocs/cfpmn/pmn.cfm?ID=K143236
Clearance of this one assay did not establish the performance of all Theranos tests.

=== layout: one-panel

# Timeline of Theranos (3 of 3) - Part 1

@body

- October 2016\: Theranos investor sues the company; Theranos downsizes
- November 2016\: Walgreens sues Theranos
- January 2017\: More layoffs, followed by a failed lab inspection
- March 2018\: Theranos is charged with massive fraud
- May 2018\: “Bad Blood” book is released
- June 2018\: Holmes and Balwani indicted
- September 2018\: Theranos is forced to close

=== layout: one-panel

# Timeline of Theranos (3 of 3) - Part 2

@body

- March 2019\: Theranos gets documentary
- June 2019\: Trial scheduled for July 2020
- August 2020\: The Holmes and Balwani trials have been severed–legalspeak for separated
- December 2020\: Due to COVID Holmes’ criminal trial delayed til 2021
- March 12, 2021\: Ms. Holmes tells the court she is pregnant, delaying her criminal trial once again
- July 2021\: Ms. Holmes gives birth to a baby boy
- August 31, 2021\: Trial finally begins after delays

=== layout: section

# The Characters

=== layout: two-panels

# Elizabeth Holmes

@left

- Founder and CEO of Theranos
  - Forbes had named Holmes the youngest and wealthiest self-made female billionaire in America on the basis of a \$9-billion valuation of her company
- Jan 3, 2022: convicted of four investor-fraud counts.
- Nov 18, 2022: sentenced to 135 months (11 years, 3 months).

@right

![Slide image 3](assets/lect05c-theranos/image_003.png)

@notes

Historical sentencing, not a claim about a current release date.
https://www.justice.gov/usao-ndca/us-v-elizabeth-holmes-et-al

=== layout: two-panels

# Ramesh "Sunny" Balwani

@left

![Slide image 4](assets/lect05c-theranos/image_004.png)

@right

- President and chief operating officer; joined in 2009.
- Managed day-to-day operations without a biological sciences background.
- Holmes's romantic partner during his tenure.
- Convicted of fraud involving investors and patients.
- Dec 7, 2022: sentenced to 155 months (12 years, 11 months).

@notes

Historical sentencing source:
https://www.justice.gov/usao-ndca/us-v-elizabeth-holmes-et-al

=== layout: two-panels

# George Shultz

@left

- former secretary of state and chairman of the Theranos board

@right

![Slide image 5](assets/lect05c-theranos/image_005.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# Tyler Shultz

@left

![Slide image 6](assets/lect05c-theranos/image_006.png)

![Slide image 7](assets/lect05c-theranos/image_007.png)

@right

- Grandson of George Shultz
- whistleblower on Theranos problems
  - served as a confidential source for a 2015 Wall Street Journal report that showed the company's device was based on lies
- Grandfather George tried to force Tyler to sign NDA and give up the story

=== layout: two-panels

# Erika Cheung

@left

- lab associate at Theranos
  - chemist and biologist degrees from Berkeley
- Main whistleblower on Theranos problems
  - Holmes and Sunny hired private investigators to follow her around
- After leaving created an organization called Ethics in Entrepreneurship

@right

![Slide image 8](assets/lect05c-theranos/image_008.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# David Boies

@left

![Slide image 9](assets/lect05c-theranos/image_009.png)

@right

- “pitbull” lawyer
- represented Al Gore during vote recount in 2000
- lead prosecution of U.S. v Microsoft
- helped overturn California Prop 8 that had banned gay marriage
- represented Harvey Weinstein for his sexual allegations 

=== layout: two-panels

# Channing Robertson

@left

- Stanford professor who first encouraged Holmes
  - connected Holmes with other rich white men to fund Theranos

@right

![Slide image 10](assets/lect05c-theranos/image_010.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# Phyllis Gardner

@left

![Slide image 11](assets/lect05c-theranos/image_011.png)

@right

- Professor of Medicine at the Stanford University School of Medicine
- skeptical of the Theranos founder Elizabeth Holmes early on
  - knew from the moment the 19-year-old student started talking about her “brilliant” idea that it wouldn't work
- rips Theranos board of 'old men' whose 'brains go to their groin' ·

=== layout: two-panels

# Rupert Murdoch

@left

- Owner of the Wall Street Journal newspaper as well as Fox News
- Major investor in Theranos
- Theranos repeatedly asked Murdoch to stop the WSJ story but he declined

@right

![Slide image 12](assets/lect05c-theranos/image_012.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# Dr. Richard Fuisz, MD

@left

![Slide image 13](assets/lect05c-theranos/image_013.png)

@right

- Childhood neighbor of Elizabeth Holmes parents
- Held a grudge against the Holmes family
- Theranos sued him, alleging that Fuisz had misappropriated a Theranos patent and used that information to file his own medical analyzer patent
  - the lawsuit eventually uncovered some problems at Theranos

=== layout: two-panels

# Avie Tevanian

@left

- the investor from Apple who stepped down
- was on the Theranos board, but asked to many questions
  - thus he was forced to resign

@right

![Slide image 14](assets/lect05c-theranos/image_014.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# John Carreyrou

@left

![Slide image 15](assets/lect05c-theranos/image_015.png)

![Slide image 16](assets/lect05c-theranos/image_016.png)

@right

- Journalist that was first to expose Theranos scandal to the world
  - in a series of Wall Street Journal articles
- Author of Bad Blood book
- Holmes and Sunny tracked his movements to avoid him getting secrets
- Hosted the trial-reporting podcast Bad Blood\: The Final Chapter

